{"candidates":[{"content":"The 'Reliability-Compliance Paradox' integrates SRC_001 and SRC_004 by proposing that the very mechanism that increases reliability (low-variance additive process) simultaneously increases the risk of 'blind compliance' (treating the checklist as a ritual). It is refuted if the rate of 'lapse errors' decreases without a corresponding increase in 'compliance-driven' false positives when the checklist is expanded.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0a07b5048a67c4213e4e113cc978ea24c78a2e1e847b7b43e5b18a021b1dc074"},{"role":"dependence","target":"099a3bb5ce1903af7536de18a8c4c6a58bb2fef89caec4e8e0119db7fa6b9cf8"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"The 'Epistemic Boundary' hypothesis abstracts SRC_002 and SRC_003 into a single principle: the relative reliability of a panel versus an expert depends on whether the 'objectively verifiable outcome' is a first-order property (observable) or a second-order property (synthetic). This relation is refuted if a non-expert panel outperforms an expert on a task requiring high-dimensional synthesis of non-linear variables.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"5d003bb87b08cf8a385a535ef9316fb8941d8abbafdcf3dedf14bb18a2cc8357"},{"role":"dependence","target":"931f8d687b0a0429f9fbecbab00212877d5e59e0651c30c85b2ad88976193bfa"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"The 'Weighting Failure' claim contradicts SRC_001 by asserting that the 'Error Cancellation Mechanism' is a fallacy in complex domains because it assumes binary checks are independent. In reality, checklist items share latent dependencies that cause non-experts to fail collectively. This is refuted if a panel's reliability remains stable as the inter-dependency of checklist items increases.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0a07b5048a67c4213e4e113cc978ea24c78a2e1e847b7b43e5b18a021b1dc074"},{"role":"dependence","target":"d36c185071e00b2be48ab692c428ef9c19852ca47861933528f84363962dcf9f"}],"typicality":0.2},{"content":"The 'Systemic Blind Spot' mechanism reduces SRC_004 to a subset of SRC_005, arguing that 'knowledge errors' are actually just failures in the implicit weighting of information. A non-expert panel doesn't just miss a step; they lack the 'pivot variable' detection. This is refuted if a panel of non-experts can correctly identify the 'pivot' variable through purely additive checklist logic.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"099a3bb5ce1903af7536de18a8c4c6a58bb2fef89caec4e8e0119db7fa6b9cf8"},{"role":"dependence","target":"d36c185071e00b2be48ab692c428ef9c19852ca47861933528f84363962dcf9f"}],"typicality":0.5},{"content":"The 'Cognitive Compression' theory proposes that SRC_003 is compatible with SRC_001 if we view the expert's intuition as a 'compiled' checklist. The expert is more reliable not because they avoid the checklist, but because they execute a higher-fidelity, internal version of it. This is refuted if an expert fails a task that is trivially solved by a literal, linear application of the checklist.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0a07b5048a67c4213e4e113cc978ea24c78a2e1e847b7b43e5b18a021b1dc074"},{"role":"dependence","target":"931f8d687b0a0429f9fbecbab00212877d5e59e0651c30c85b2ad88976193bfa"}],"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}